-- title:
--   Polynomial contact ideals for the two elliptic-extension charts
-- statement:
--   For complex parameters $g_2,g_3$, consider the two polynomial derivations on four-variable polynomial rings over $\mathbb C$:
--
--   $$\mathcal D_0=\partial_t+y\partial_x+(6x^2-g_2/2)\partial_y-x\partial_r$$
--
--   in variables $(t,x,y,r)$, and
--
--   $$\mathcal D_2=\partial_t+(-6b^2+g_2a^2/2)\partial_a
--   +(-1/2-g_2ab-3g_3a^2/2)\partial_b
--   +(-2g_2b^2-3g_3ab)\partial_d$$
--
--   in variables $(t,a,b,d)$.
--
--   For either chart, a point $v\in\mathbb C^4$ and an integer $N\ge0$, define the contact ideal
--
--   $$J_{c,v}(N)=\left\langle p\in\mathbb C[x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3] :
--   (\mathcal D_c^k p)(v)=0\text{ for every }0\le k<N\right\rangle.$$
--
--   Angle brackets mean the ideal generated by the displayed set. The definition uses the ambient four-variable polynomial ring and an arbitrary affine evaluation point. Neither the cubic equation nor a nonzero chart denominator is required. The assertion that the generating set is already an ideal, and all structural and interpolation properties, are separate theorem obligations.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The chart indices `0` and `1` refer to the homogeneous-coordinate charts 0 and 2. The order $N=0$ imposes no jet conditions.
-- source:
--   Explicit ideal-span definition for the polynomial chart jet vanishing conditions arising from Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, Theorem A.2 and the elliptic-extension curve of Appendix A.2, https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. This is derived formalization infrastructure, not a new axiom or a quoted quantitative theorem. Structural properties are separate theorem obligations.

import Definitions.Def_WeierstrassEllipticZeta_ProjectiveChartCalculus
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Span

noncomputable section
namespace WeierstrassEllipticZeta
open MvPolynomial

/-- The ideal generated by polynomial functions whose first `n` chart jets
vanish at an affine point. Closure of the generating set is a theorem. -/
def extensionChartContactIdeal (g₂ g₃ : ℂ) (c : Fin 2) (v : Fin 4 → ℂ)
    (n : ℕ) : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ) :=
  Ideal.span {p | ∀ k < n, eval v ((extensionChartDerivation g₂ g₃ c)^[k] p) = 0}

end WeierstrassEllipticZeta


